export default "## Why Users Are Leaving My Calendar\n\nMy Calendar by Abishkking is a simple period tracker on Google Play. It offers basic cycle tracking without the feature bloat of larger apps. For users who want something straightforward, that simplicity is appealing.\n\nThe problem is not the features. It is the lack of documentation about what happens to your data.\n\nMy Calendar provides limited privacy documentation. For a general utility app, that might be acceptable. For an app that records your menstrual cycle, fertility windows, and sexual activity, limited documentation is a serious gap. Users cannot evaluate what they do not know, and what is undocumented is effectively unaccountable.\n\nThe app is ad-supported, which means third-party ad SDKs are embedded in the application. These SDKs collect device identifiers and behavioral data to serve targeted advertising. When the behavioral data includes reproductive health patterns, the stakes of that data collection change substantially.\n\n## What My Calendar Does With Your Data\n\nThree data concerns apply to My Calendar.\n\nAd SDK data collection is inherent to the ad-supported model. Third-party advertising SDKs collect device advertising identifiers, IP addresses, and app usage patterns. This data flows to ad networks where it is used for targeting and may be combined with data from other sources to build user profiles.\n\nOptional cloud sync introduces server-side storage. If enabled, your period data is transmitted to and stored on remote servers. Server-stored data is subject to the developer's data retention policies (which are not clearly documented), legal process including subpoenas, and potential security breaches.\n\nSmall development teams present a specific governance concern. Larger companies, despite their own privacy failures, typically have dedicated security teams, incident response procedures, and legal compliance resources. Smaller teams may not have the infrastructure to protect sensitive health data at the same standard. This is not a criticism of small developers. It is a structural reality of data stewardship. See our guide on [how period tracker apps collect data](/resources/guides/how-period-tracker-apps-collect-data).\n\n## What Changes When You Switch to On-Device Storage\n\nLocal-first storage reduces the documentation problem. When core records stay on your phone, you need less trust in a privacy policy, a development team's security practices, or a company's data governance.\n\nFloriva keeps core period data on your device. No ad SDKs. No account for core tracking. Optional sync sends only end-to-end encrypted data Floriva cannot read. Learn more about [what a zero-knowledge period tracker means](/resources/guides/what-is-zero-knowledge-period-tracker).\n\n## How to Switch\n\nSwitching from My Calendar to Floriva takes minutes:\n\n1. Note your recent period dates and cycle lengths from My Calendar.\n2. Install Floriva, no account needed.\n3. Enter your cycle history to establish predictions.\n4. Disable cloud sync in My Calendar if enabled, then delete the app.\n\nFor a comparison of private trackers, see our guide to the [best private period tracker apps](/resources/best/best-private-period-tracker-apps).\n\n## The Privacy Bottom Line\n\nMy Calendar's limited privacy documentation means users are trusting an ad-supported app with reproductive health data without clear information about where that data goes. If you want a period tracker where the answer to \"what happens to my data\" is unambiguous (it stays on your device), you need an app with a different architecture.";
